A Theory of Decision-Validated Learning Gates for Budgeted Scientific Discovery

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

This programme specifies a theoretical and measurement-theoretic study of the learning gate used by Autonomous Science Bench (ASB) and its descendants. No physical experiments are performed and no code is trained. The object of study is the relation between two quantities: the ranking metric  \mathrm{NDCG}@k , on which the gate currently operates, and the downstream selection regret  R_k , which is the decision-theoretic quantity the gate is intended to certify. The central claim is that these two quantities are not order-equivalent, that the gap between them is quantifiable, and that a gate stated directly on  R_k  is both necessary and statistically implementable under explicitly stated assumptions.

---

1. The finite-pool decision problem and the non-identifiability of ranking as decision

Let  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}} = \{x_1,\dots,x_m\}  be a finite evaluation pool,  f:\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}\to\mathbb R  the unknown response, and  k  a downstream batch size. A predictor  \hat f:\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}\to\mathbb R  induces a top- k  set  T(\hat f) . The realized selection regret is

R_k(\hat f;f) := \frac{1}{k}\left[\max_{\substack{T\subseteq\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}\\ |T|=k}}\sum_{x\in T}f(x) \;-\; \sum_{x\in T(\hat f)}f(x)\right]\in[0,\operatorname{range}_{\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}}(f)].

The seed-only baseline  \hat f_0 , trained on the public seed  \mathcal D_0  alone, provides the floor  R_k(\hat f_0;f)  that any predictor must improve upon in decision terms.

ASB’s learning gate passes  \hat f  iff  \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f)\ge \tau^\star , with relevance  \rho(y)=\max(y-\tau,0)  and

\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f)=\frac{1}{Z_k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\rho\big(y_{\pi(i)}\big)}{\log_2(i+1)},

where  \pi  orders  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}  by decreasing  \hat f , and  Z_k  is the ideal DCG for the same  f  and  k . The threshold  \tau^\star  is calibrated by the reference-margin procedure; no decision quantity appears in this calibration.

Proposition 1 (NDCG does not determine selection regret). There exist a pool of size  m=5 ,  k=2 , a response vector  f , a relevance offset  \tau=0 , and two predictors  \hat f_1,\hat f_2  such that

\mathrm{NDCG}@2(\hat f_1;f)=\mathrm{NDCG}@2(\hat f_2;f)
\quad\text{and}\quad
R_2(\hat f_1;f)\neq R_2(\hat f_2;f).

Construction. Let  f=(10,\,8,\,3.17,\,0,\,0) . The ideal top-two DCG is  10+8/\log_2 3\approx 15.047 , so  Z_2=15.047  and the optimal batch mean is  9 . Define  \hat f_1  by scores  (10,9,0,8,0)  and  \hat f_2  by scores  (0,10,9,0,0) . Then  T(\hat f_1)=\{x_1,x_4\} ,  T(\hat f_2)=\{x_2,x_3\} . Both attain DCG  =10  and hence NDCG  \approx 0.6646 . Their regrets are  4  and  3.415 , respectively.  \square 

Proposition 1 establishes that the map  \hat f\mapsto \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)  is not injective on the equivalence classes of  T(\hat f) . This is a metric property; its empirical force depends on whether the reachable predictor class  \mathcal H , induced by a campaign’s reference solution and measurement budget, contains predictors whose NDCG-regret gap is non-negligible. The literature on inter-metric regret transfer confirms the structural asymmetry: bounded regret on one metric does not guarantee bounded regret on another without a margin condition, and listwise metrics such as NDCG are particularly vulnerable to this asymmetry.

The programme therefore studies the gap functional

\Delta_k(\hat f;f):=R_k(\hat f;f)\;-\;\inf\big\{R_k(\hat f';f):\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f';f)\ge \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)\big\},

which measures the excess regret paid by a particular predictor among all predictors with at least as good an NDCG. A bound of the form  \Delta_k\le C\cdot \mathrm{Var}(f\text{ near top-}k)/\delta_k^2 , where  \delta_k  is the top- k  margin, is the natural theoretical target.

