2. Conditional perturbation bounds and the limits of worst-case analysis

Proposition 2 (Global  \ell_\infty  bound). If  \|\hat f-f\|_\infty\le\eta  on  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}} , then  R_k(\hat f;f)\le 2\eta .

Proof. Let  T^\star  be optimal under  f  and  T_{\hat f}=T(\hat f) . Since  T_{\hat f}  maximizes  \sum\hat f  over size- k  subsets,

\sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}f(x)\ge \sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}\hat f(x)-k\eta \ge \sum_{x\in T^\star}\hat f(x)-k\eta \ge \sum_{x\in T^\star}f(x)-2k\eta.

Dividing by  k  gives the claim.  \square 

The bound is tight at  k=1 . At campaign scale,  \eta  estimated from a posterior fit on a budget of a few hundred measurements is typically  0.1 – 0.3  in normalized relevance, so  2\eta  is  0.2 – 0.6 , which exceeds any target decision tolerance on several campaigns. Proposition 2 is therefore true but uninformative at the scale where the benchmark operates. This motivates the localized version.

Proposition 3 (Localized  \ell_\infty  bound). Let  \eta_T := \max_{x\in T(\hat f)\cup T^\star}|\hat f(x)-f(x)| . Then  R_k(\hat f;f)\le 2\eta_T .

Proof. Identical to Proposition 2 with  \eta  replaced by  \eta_T  on the union of the two batches.  \square 

The quantity  \eta_T  is estimable from the delivered predictor and the agent’s own measurements on the designs it actually compares.

Proposition 4 (Margin condition for zero regret). If  \|\hat f-f\|_\infty\le\eta  and the top- k  margin satisfies  \delta_k := f(x_{(k)})-f(x_{(k+1)})>2\eta , then  T(\hat f)=T^\star  and  R_k(\hat f;f)=0 .

Proof. For  x\in T^\star  and  y\notin T^\star ,

\hat f(x)-\hat f(y)\ge f(x)-f(y)-2\eta\ge \delta_k-2\eta>0.

Every true top- k  design strictly dominates every non-top- k  design under  \hat f .  \square 

The constant  2\eta , not  \eta/2 , is correct; equality is attained at  \delta_k=2\eta  in the  m=2,k=1  example. Proposition 4 is expected to apply where response peaks are separated and to fail where relevant designs cluster. Its failure is an empirical variable, measured per campaign as  (\hat\eta_T,\hat\delta_k,\hat\delta_k-2\hat\eta_T) , not a defect of the theory.

