3. The decision gate: specification and statistical validity

The programme replaces the NDCG threshold with

G^{\mathrm{dec}}_{k_{\mathrm{dec}},\varepsilon_c}(\hat f)=\mathbf 1\!\left[\,U_{1-\delta}\big(R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f;f)\big)\le \varepsilon_c\,\right],

where  U_{1-\delta}  is a one-sided upper confidence bound and  k_{\mathrm{dec}}  is a decision batch size chosen independently of the NDCG window  k_{\mathrm{NDCG}} . The two cutoffs must be separated:  k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}  is the ranking window of the benchmark’s metric (20 or 100), while  k_{\mathrm{dec}}  is the number of designs a deployed predictor would select next, set to the campaign’s batch size  b  and varied over  \{b,k_{\mathrm{NDCG}},\lfloor m/10\rfloor\}  in sensitivity analysis.

For campaign  c  with released splits  S_c  of size  n_c , the tolerance is defined as

\varepsilon_c(\rho):=\frac{1-\rho}{n_c}\sum_{s\in S_c}\frac{R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f_{0,s};f_s)}{\operatorname{range}_{\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}}(f_s)},

where  \hat f_{0,s}  is the seed-only predictor fitted on split  s , and  \rho\in(0,1)  is the required fractional improvement over the seed-only baseline. This is a split average of a normalized realized quantity, not a posterior expectation under the programme’s own surrogate; it is reproducible from the released splits without a modeling commitment. The value  \rho=1/2  is chosen as the smallest improvement not within the sampling noise of the benchmark’s split counts, and the programme pre-commits to reporting  G^{\mathrm{dec}}  for  \rho\in\{1/4,1/2,3/4\} .

The quantity the benchmark itself implicitly certifies is

\varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}:=\sup\big\{R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f;f_s):\mathrm{NDCG}@k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}(\hat f;f_s)=\tau^\star_c,\ s\in S_c\big\},

the largest decision regret compatible with sitting exactly at the benchmark’s NDCG threshold on the released splits. The values  \{\varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}\}_{c}  answer a question the benchmark does not answer: what decision regret did the NDCG threshold actually certify?

The confidence bound  U_{1-\delta}  is a single procedure whose regime of validity is stated. For  n_c\ge 24 , it is the bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap upper quantile over splits. BCa is second-order accurate for smooth statistics and is the gold-standard general-purpose interval for small to moderate samples; because  R_k  is piecewise constant in the ranks, the programme reports the jackknife acceleration and a coverage check on synthetic pools where the truth is known. For  n_c<24 , no frequentist bound is claimed: the programme reports the posterior-mean  \overline R_k  and the descriptive min–max range over the  n_c  catalog orders, labelled explicitly as not a confidence bound. The decomposition adopted is

R_k(\hat f;f)\;\le\;\overline R_k(\hat f\mid \mathcal D)\;+\;\underbrace{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{MC}}}_{\text{finite sampling}}\;+\;\underbrace{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}}_{\text{not covered by theory}}.

The term  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}  is the bias from surrogate misspecification. It is not bounded by any theorem in this programme; it is estimated by held-out calibration of posterior predictive intervals and reported as an explicit bias budget. If  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}  exceeds  \varepsilon_c  on a campaign, the decision gate is vacuous there, and the programme reports that as a negative result.

