4. Conjectures at the boundary of the theory

Two claims are deliberately left as conjectures with stated proof strategies and counterexample searches.

Conjecture A (no uniform threshold across pool sizes). For any  t\in(0,1)  and any  \varepsilon>0 , there exist  m\ge k+2 , a pool  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}  of size  m , and  f,\hat f  with  \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)\ge t  and  R_k(\hat f;f)/\operatorname{range}(f)\ge\varepsilon . The intended proof fixes the ratio  \delta/\operatorname{range}(f)  and lets  k  grow so that the NDCG deficit from one mis-ranked boundary design is absorbed below  1-t , while the regret remains a fixed fraction of the range. A naive scaling argument in which  f  is multiplied by a constant is invalid, because NDCG is scale-invariant and normalized regret is unchanged.

Conjecture B (conditional monotonicity). Define

\tau_k^{\mathrm{emp}}(\varepsilon):=\inf\big\{t:\sup_{\hat f:\,\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)=t}R_k(\hat f;f)\le\varepsilon\big\}

for fixed  f  and fixed  k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}=k . Under a fixed margin profile  \{\delta_j\}_{j\ge 1} ,  \tau_k^{\mathrm{emp}}  is non-decreasing in  k . The monotonicity is not claimed unconditionally: enlarging  k  also enlarges the optimal batch and lowers its mean, so average regret can fall even as the number of near-ties crossed grows. The proof strategy conditions on the margin profile and controls the denominator; the counterexample search fixes the margin profile and varies the tail of  f  to force a decrease.

The NDCG-derived threshold  \tau^\star  as an infimum over all predictors with a given NDCG is not a defined object: Proposition 1 shows the qualifying set can be empty for every  t<1 . The programme therefore does not define  \tau^\star  and does not claim monotonicity for it. The decision gate of §3 is the only threshold the programme endorses.

---

5. The measurement programme

The theory is conditional on quantities that must be estimated. Four measurement targets are specified, none requiring new physical experiments.

(M1) The implicit tolerance table. Compute  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}  for the six paper campaigns from the released pools, thresholds, and reference predictors. This is the primary empirical output and the first direct test of whether the benchmark’s NDCG thresholds certify a coherent decision tolerance.

(M2) The margin diagnostic. For each campaign and each released predictor, estimate  (\hat\eta_T,\hat\delta_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}})  and report whether Proposition 4’s condition  \hat\delta_k>2\hat\eta_T  holds. This locates where the NDCG gate and the decision gate are expected to agree (separated landscapes) and where they are expected to diverge (clustered landscapes).

(M3) The gap profile. On synthetic pools matched to each campaign’s empirical value distribution and split structure, compute  \Delta_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}  across a grid of predictors in the reachable class  \mathcal H . The output is a curve of excess regret versus NDCG, quantifying how badly a high NDCG can mislead within the class the benchmark actually admits.

(M4) The  \rho -sensitivity profile. Report the decision-gate pass/fail matrix across configurations for  \rho\in\{1/4,1/2,3/4\} , and report whether the ordering of configurations is stable. Instability in  \rho  is itself a finding: it would show that the decision tolerance, not the predictor, determines the verdict.

Targets M1 and M2 require the delivered predictors or the released pools from which they can be reconstructed. If the predictor artefacts are not recoverable from the benchmark’s public release, the programme restricts M1 and M2 to the reference and seed-only predictors, which are constructed from public data, and treats M3 as the primary evidence. This restriction is stated in advance and does not change the falsification criteria.

---

6. Falsification and the logical status of the thesis

The programme is falsifiable in three places, and the falsifiers are stated before any measurement.

F1 (empirical adequacy of the heuristic). If the implicit tolerances  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}  lie within a factor of  1.5  of one another across the six campaigns, the benchmark’s NDCG calibration is empirically decision-coherent, and the programme pivots to a structural explanation of why.

F2 (class-level irrelevance of Proposition 1). If, on synthetic pools matched to each campaign and restricted to the reachable predictor class  \mathcal H , the gap  \Delta_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}  is below the sampling noise on at least five of six campaigns, then the non-identifiability of Proposition 1 does not bite in the regime the benchmark operates, and the empirical contribution contracts to the tolerance table.

F3 (instability in  \rho ). If the decision-gate pass/fail ordering of the evaluated configurations reverses between  \rho=1/4  and  \rho=3/4  on more than two campaigns, then the decision tolerance dominates the verdict, and the programme reports the gate as tolerance-dependent rather than as a unique certification.

If any two of F1–F3 trigger, the thesis becomes a formal diagnosis of NDCG-as-learning-gate together with evidence that the benchmark’s heuristic was adequate on its six campaigns for identifiable structural reasons. This is a legitimate and publishable outcome, written into the programme as Plan B.

Proved. Proposition 1 (NDCG does not determine selection regret); Proposition 2 (global perturbation bound, tight at  k=1 ); Proposition 3 (localized perturbation bound); Proposition 4 (margin condition for zero regret, constant  2\eta ).

Specified. The decision gate  G^{\mathrm{dec}} , the split-level tolerance  \varepsilon_c(\rho) , the implicit benchmark tolerance  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}} , and the three-term uncertainty decomposition with model misspecification explicitly outside the theory.

Open, with proof strategies and counterexample searches. Conjecture A (no uniform threshold across pool sizes); Conjecture B (conditional monotonicity); the gap bound  \Delta_k\le C\cdot\mathrm{Var}(f\text{ near top-}k)/\delta_k^2 .

Explicitly not claimed. A universal NDCG-to-regret threshold; unconditional monotonicity of any calibration in  k , pool size, or hit rate; a frequentist guarantee for  n<24 ; a bound on model misspecification; and any empirical result, since no experiments have been run.

The thesis’s single sentence is:

NDCG@ k  does not determine the selection regret of a predictor, and a learning gate that certifies decision adequacy must be stated as a bound on that regret; the programme proves the non-identifiability, proves the conditional bounds that survive, specifies a statistically honest replacement, and pre-registers the measurements that would show whether the existing benchmark’s heuristic was, on its six campaigns, accidentally adequate or substantively misleading.

