A.5 What Proposition 1 establishes and does not establish

Establishes. The map  \hat f\mapsto \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)  is not injective on the equivalence classes of  T(\hat f) . Two predictors with identical NDCG@ k  can induce different top- k  selections with different regret. This is a property of the metric, independent of any particular predictor class.

Does not establish. It does not show that no threshold  t  works on a given pool for a restricted predictor class. On a specific campaign, it may happen that all plausible predictors lie in a regime where NDCG and regret co-vary. The empirical force of Proposition 1 therefore depends on the reachable class  \mathcal H  defined in Appendix I. This distinction is consistent with the inter-metric regret-transfer literature, which shows that bounded regret on one metric does not guarantee bounded regret on another without a margin condition, and that listwise metrics such as NDCG are particularly vulnerable to this asymmetry. The theoretical analysis of NDCG further shows that standard NDCG with logarithmic discount converges to  1  as the number of ranked items grows, which implies that the metric’s discriminative power is structurally limited at large pool sizes.

A.6 Predictor existence

The predictors  \hat f_1,\hat f_2  are explicitly constructed as score functions in §A.2. They are not abstract selectors; they are real-valued functions on  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}} . The construction uses scores in  \{0,8,9,10\} , so both predictors are bounded and well-defined. No Gaussian-process training is assumed; Proposition 1 is a statement about the metric, not about a training procedure.

