Appendix B. Proofs of Propositions 2, 3, and 4

B.1 Proposition 2 (Global  \ell_\infty  bound)

Proposition 2. If  \|\hat f-f\|_\infty\le\eta  on  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}} , then  R_k(\hat f;f)\le 2\eta .

Proof. Let  T^\star  be an optimal size- k  subset under  f , and let  T_{\hat f}=T(\hat f)  be the top- k  set under  \hat f . By definition of  T_{\hat f}  as the maximizer of  \sum_{x\in T}\hat f(x)  over size- k  subsets,

\sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}\hat f(x)\ge \sum_{x\in T^\star}\hat f(x).

Using  |\hat f(x)-f(x)|\le\eta  for every  x :

\sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}f(x)
\ge
\sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}\hat f(x)-k\eta
\ge
\sum_{x\in T^\star}\hat f(x)-k\eta
\ge
\sum_{x\in T^\star}f(x)-2k\eta.

Therefore

\sum_{x\in T^\star}f(x)-\sum_{x\in T_{\hat f}}f(x)\le 2k\eta.

Dividing by  k  gives  R_k(\hat f;f)\le 2\eta .  \square 

Tightness at  k=1 . Let  m=2 ,  k=1 ,  f(x_1)=0 ,  f(x_2)=-2\eta ,  \hat f(x_1)=-\eta ,  \hat f(x_2)=0 . Then  \|\hat f-f\|_\infty=\eta . The predictor selects  x_2  (since  \hat f(x_2)=0>-\eta=\hat f(x_1) ), but  x_1  is optimal under  f . The regret is

R_1(\hat f;f)=\frac{1}{1}\big(0-(-2\eta)\big)=2\eta,

so the bound is attained.

No general- k  tightness is claimed. The product construction requires independence across pairs and is not valid on a fixed pool, because the predictor’s choice on one pair may interact with its choice on another.

B.2 Proposition 3 (Localized  \ell_\infty  bound)

Proposition 3. Let  \eta_T := \max_{x\in T(\hat f)\cup T^\star}|\hat f(x)-f(x)| . Then  R_k(\hat f;f)\le 2\eta_T .

Proof. The proof is identical to Proposition 2 with  \eta  replaced by  \eta_T  on the union of the two batches. For every  x\in T_{\hat f}\cup T^\star ,  |\hat f(x)-f(x)|\le\eta_T . The chain of inequalities in §B.1 remains valid with  \eta_T  in place of  \eta .  \square 

The significance of Proposition 3 is that  \eta_T  is estimable from the delivered predictor and the agent’s own measurements on the designs it actually compares, whereas the global  \eta  over all  m  designs is not.

B.3 Proposition 4 (Margin condition for zero regret)

Proposition 4. If  \|\hat f-f\|_\infty\le\eta  and the top- k  margin satisfies

\delta_k := f(x_{(k)})-f(x_{(k+1)})>2\eta,

then  T(\hat f)=T^\star  and  R_k(\hat f;f)=0 .

Proof. Let  x\in T^\star  and  y\notin T^\star . Since  x  is in the true top- k  and  y  is not,  f(x)\ge f(x_{(k)})  and  f(y)\le f(x_{(k+1)}) . Therefore

f(x)-f(y)\ge f(x_{(k)})-f(x_{(k+1)})=\delta_k.

Using the  \ell_\infty  bound,

\hat f(x)-\hat f(y)
\ge
f(x)-f(y)-2\eta
\ge
\delta_k-2\eta
>
0.

Hence every true top- k  design strictly dominates every non-top- k  design under  \hat f . Consequently, the top- k  set under  \hat f  is exactly  T^\star , and the regret is zero.  \square 

