\frac{1-\rho}{n_c}
\sum_{s\in S_c}
\frac{R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f_{0,s};f_s)}
{\operatorname{range}_{\mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}}(f_s)},

where  \hat f_{0,s}  is the seed-only predictor fitted on split  s , and  \rho\in(0,1)  is the required fractional improvement over the seed-only baseline. The programme pre-commits to reporting the gate for

\rho\in\{1/4,\;1/2,\;3/4\}.

The value  \rho=1/2  is the primary specification; the other values are sensitivity checks.

C.4 Implicit benchmark tolerance  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}} 

The quantity the benchmark itself implicitly certifies is

\varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}
:=
\sup
\left\{
R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f;f_s)
:
\mathrm{NDCG}@k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}(\hat f;f_s)=\tau^\star_c,
\; s\in S_c
\right\},

the largest decision regret compatible with sitting exactly at the benchmark’s NDCG threshold on the released splits. This answers the question: what decision regret did the NDCG threshold actually certify?

C.5 Bootstrap procedure

For  n_c\ge 24 . The upper bound  U_{1-\delta}  is the bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap upper quantile over splits. BCa is second-order accurate for smooth statistics; because  R_k  is piecewise constant in the ranks, the programme reports the jackknife acceleration and a coverage check on synthetic pools where the truth is known. The use of bootstrap for top- K  selection and rank confidence intervals is supported by the ranking-inference literature, which establishes comprehensive inference tools grounded in asymptotic normality and bootstrapping techniques for top- K  selection and rank confidence interval construction.

For  n_c<24 . No frequentist bound is claimed. The programme reports the posterior-mean  \overline R_k  and the descriptive min–max range over the  n_c  catalog orders, labelled explicitly as not a confidence bound. This is preferable to a parametric bootstrap that would be entirely model-dependent and would contradict the uncertainty decomposition of §C.6.

C.6 Uncertainty decomposition

The programme adopts the decomposition

R_k(\hat f;f)
\;\le\;
\overline R_k(\hat f\mid \mathcal D)
\;+\;
\underbrace{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{MC}}}_{\text{finite sampling}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}}_{\text{not covered by theory}}.

The term  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{MC}}  is controlled by Monte-Carlo sample size. The term  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}  is the bias from surrogate misspecification. It is not bounded by any theorem in this programme; it is estimated by held-out calibration of posterior predictive intervals and reported as an explicit bias budget. If  \varepsilon_{\mathrm{model}}  exceeds  \varepsilon_c  on a campaign, the decision gate is vacuous there, and the programme reports that as a negative result.

---

Appendix D. The Gap Functional  \Delta_k  and Its Estimation

D.1 Definition

The gap functional is

\Delta_k(\hat f;f)
:=
R_k(\hat f;f)
\;-\;
\inf
\left\{
R_k(\hat f';f)
:
\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f';f)\ge \mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)
\right\}.

It measures the excess regret paid by a particular predictor among all predictors with at least as good an NDCG.

D.2 Lower-envelope construction

For a finite set of predictors  \{\hat f_1,\dots,\hat f_N\} , compute  (\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f_j),\ R_k(\hat f_j;f))  for each  j . Sort the points by NDCG. The lower envelope is

E(t)
:=
\min
\left\{
R_k(\hat f_j;f)
:
\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f_j;f)\ge t
\right\}.

Then

\Delta_k(\hat f_j;f)
=
R_k(\hat f_j;f)-E\big(\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f_j;f)\big).

This is the operational definition used in the simulation of Appendix J.

D.3 Interpretation

A large  \Delta_k  means that the predictor’s NDCG is achieved by many other predictors with lower regret, so the predictor is paying an excess decision penalty relative to its ranking quality. A small  \Delta_k  means that the predictor’s NDCG is near the best achievable regret at that NDCG level.

The programme’s theoretical target is a bound of the form

\Delta_k\le C\cdot \frac{\mathrm{Var}(f\text{ near top-}k)}{\delta_k^2},

which would make the gap controllable by landscape roughness and margin. This bound is open; it is the natural target of the programme’s third year.

