Appendix E. Conjecture A — No Uniform Threshold Across Pool Sizes

E.1 Formal statement

Conjecture A. For any  t\in(0,1)  and any  \varepsilon>0 , there exist  m\ge k+2 , a pool  \mathcal X_{\mathrm{eval}}  of size  m , and  f,\hat f  with

\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)\ge t
\quad\text{and}\quad
\frac{R_k(\hat f;f)}{\operatorname{range}(f)}\ge\varepsilon.

E.2 Structural mechanism

The failure of a uniform threshold  t  stems from the asymptotic properties of the logarithmic discount weights

w(i)=\frac{1}{\log_2(i+1)}.

Let the true evaluation pool be sorted so that  f(x_{(1)})\ge \cdots \ge f(x_{(m)}) . The ideal DCG is

Z_k(f)=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\rho(f(x_{(i)}))}{\log_2(i+1)}.

Consider a predictor  \hat f  that correctly identifies the top  k-1  items but replaces the  k -th optimal item  x_{(k)}  with a suboptimal item  x_{\mathrm{sub}}  of much lower response. The DCG achieved is

\mathrm{DCG}_k(\hat f)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\frac{\rho(f(x_{(i)}))}{\log_2(i+1)}
+
\frac{\rho(f(x_{\mathrm{sub}}))}{\log_2(k+1)}.

The deficit is

\Delta\mathrm{DCG}
=
\frac{\rho(f(x_{(k)}))-\rho(f(x_{\mathrm{sub}}))}{\log_2(k+1)}.

The key observation is that the denominator  \log_2(k+1)  dampens the penalty of misranking the boundary element. For fixed  k , as the pool size  m  grows, the background items can be made arbitrarily numerous and arbitrarily low without changing  k  or the top- k  structure, while the regret  R_k  includes the absolute drop  f(x_{(k)})-f(x_{\mathrm{sub}}) . If this drop is held constant (or scaled independently of the logarithmic discount), then NDCG@ k  can remain arbitrarily close to  1  while normalized regret remains bounded away from zero. This mechanism is consistent with the theoretical analysis showing that standard NDCG converges to  1  as the number of ranked items grows.

E.3 Invalid scaling argument

A naive argument in which  f  is multiplied by a large constant is invalid, because NDCG is scale-invariant (both numerator and  Z_k  scale by the same constant), and normalized regret is also scale-invariant when divided by  \operatorname{range}(f) . The conjecture must be proved by varying the pool structure and the ratio  \delta/\operatorname{range}(f)  while controlling  k , not by rescaling  f .

E.4 Proof strategy

The intended proof fixes the ratio  \delta/\operatorname{range}(f)  and lets  k  grow so that the NDCG deficit from one mis-ranked boundary design is absorbed below  1-t , while the regret remains a fixed fraction of the range. The construction must explicitly compute the NDCG limit and verify that it exceeds  t  for the chosen parameters.

E.5 If Conjecture A fails

If Conjecture A fails—meaning that  \mathrm{NDCG}@k\ge t  does strictly bound normalized regret uniformly across all valid pool structures—the failure yields a positive metatheorem: a guaranteed Lipschitz-type mapping from ranking-metric space to decision-utility space under specified range conditions. This would partially bridge the gap identified in Proposition 1, albeit at the cost of a uniform threshold. Either outcome is publishable: a proof of the conjecture is a negative result about NDCG as a decision proxy, while a disproof is a positive structural result.

---

Appendix F. Conjecture B — Conditional Monotonicity

F.1 Formal definition of the empirical threshold

For fixed  f  and fixed  k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}=k , define

\tau_k^{\mathrm{emp}}(\varepsilon)
:=
\inf
\left\{
t:
\sup_{\hat f:\,\mathrm{NDCG}@k(\hat f;f)=t}
R_k(\hat f;f)
\le
\varepsilon
\right\}.

Conjecture B. Under a fixed margin profile  \{\delta_j\}_{j\ge 1}  with  \delta_j=f(x_{(j)})-f(x_{(j+1)}) ,  \tau_k^{\mathrm{emp}}  is non-decreasing in  k .

F.2 Two competing effects

Enlarging  k  to  k+1  has two opposing effects:

1. Denominator dilution. The regret is averaged over  k+1  items rather than  k , which can lower the average.
2. Margin cross-over. The selection window expands deeper into the landscape, crossing smaller margins  \delta_{k+1},\delta_{k+2},\dots . If the landscape has a dense cluster of near-optimal designs below rank  k , a small perturbation in  \hat f  can swap multiple items near the boundary, inflating the worst-case regret.

Monotonicity is not claimed unconditionally. The conjecture asserts that under a fixed margin profile, the second effect dominates sufficiently to require a higher NDCG threshold for the same regret tolerance.

F.3 Proof strategy

Condition on the infimum margin over the active boundary:

\Delta_k^{\min}=\inf_{j\le k}\delta_j.

Case 1 (Wide margins). If  \Delta_k^{\min}>2\eta , Proposition 4 guarantees zero regret for both  k  and  k+1 , trivializing the threshold requirement.

Case 2 (Clustered margins). When  \Delta_k^{\min}  approaches zero, adding the  (k+1) -th element introduces a high-entropy boundary state where minor ranking errors drastically inflate  R_{k+1} . To suppress this inflation to match the strictness of  \varepsilon , the required NDCG threshold must be pushed higher, yielding  \tau_{k+1}^{\mathrm{emp}}(\varepsilon)\ge\tau_k^{\mathrm{emp}}(\varepsilon) .

F.4 Counterexample search

The conjecture is accompanied by a counterexample search: construct a tail of  f  where expanding  k  absorbs high-variance mediocre items into a larger batch average, paradoxically lowering the aggregate normalized regret and allowing a lower NDCG threshold to pass the decision gate. Such a construction would falsify the conjecture and delimit its scope.

F.5 Status of  \tau^\star 

The NDCG-derived threshold  \tau^\star  as an infimum over all predictors with a given NDCG is not a defined object: Proposition 1 shows that the qualifying set can be empty for every  t<1 . The programme therefore does not define  \tau^\star  and does not claim monotonicity for it. The decision gate of Appendix C is the only threshold the programme endorses.

