Appendix G. The Measurement Programme (M1–M4)

G.1 M1 — The implicit tolerance table

Objective. Compute  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}  for the six paper campaigns from the released pools, thresholds, and reference predictors.

Procedure.

1. For each campaign  c  and split  s\in S_c , extract all evaluated predictor artefacts  \hat f  satisfying  \mathrm{NDCG}@k_{\mathrm{NDCG}}(\hat f;f_s)=\tau^\star_c  within tolerance  \pm 10^{-4} .
2. Compute  R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f;f_s)  for each qualifying predictor.
3. Compute  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}=\sup_{s}\sup_{\hat f}R_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}(\hat f;f_s) .

Fallback. If complete predictor logs are unavailable, restrict to the reference solution predictors  \hat f_{\mathrm{ref},s} , yielding a lower bound on the implicit tolerance.

Output. A table across the six campaigns with split count, threshold, implicit tolerance, and regime label (frequentist-validated for  n_c\ge 24 , descriptive for  n_c<24 ).

G.2 M2 — The margin diagnostic

Objective. Locate where NDCG and decision gates are expected to agree (separated landscapes) and where they are expected to diverge (clustered landscapes).

Procedure. For each campaign and predictor, estimate

\hat\eta_T=\max_{x\in T(\hat f)\cup T^\star}|\hat f(x)-f_s(x)|

and

\hat\delta_k=f_s(x_{(k)})-f_s(x_{(k+1)}).

Classify each split into:

· Zone A (separated peak):  \hat\delta_k>2\hat\eta_T . Proposition 4 applies; regret is zero.
· Zone B (clustered hit):  \hat\delta_k\le 2\hat\eta_T . NDCG is vulnerable to non-identifiability divergence.

Output. A per-campaign classification of splits into Zone A and Zone B, and the distribution of  \hat\delta_k-2\hat\eta_T .

G.3 M3 — The gap profile

Objective. Quantify  \Delta_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}  across the reachable predictor class  \mathcal H  on synthetic pools matched to campaign distributions.

Procedure.

1. Fit a Gaussian mixture model or kernel density estimator to each campaign’s empirical value distribution  f_s  and split structure.
2. Generate  N  synthetic evaluation pools from the fitted distribution.
3. For each pool, generate a surrogate predictor class  \mathcal H  by posterior-noise perturbation (Appendix I).
4. Compute  \mathrm{NDCG}@k  and  R_k  for every  \hat f\in\mathcal H .
5. Construct the lower envelope  E(t)  and compute  \Delta_k  for each predictor.
6. Report the distribution of  \Delta_k  and the maximum observed gap.

Output. A gap-profile curve of excess regret versus NDCG for each campaign.

G.4 M4 — The  \rho -sensitivity profile

Objective. Test the stability of the decision gate’s pass/fail verdicts against variations in  \rho .

Procedure.

1. Instantiate  G^{\mathrm{dec}}  for  \rho\in\{1/4,1/2,3/4\} .
2. Compute  \varepsilon_c(\rho)  for each  \rho .
3. Generate the pass/fail binary matrix for all evaluated configurations across all campaigns for each  \rho .
4. Check whether the ordering of configurations reverses between  \rho=1/4  and  \rho=3/4 .

Output. A  \rho -sensitivity matrix and a stability verdict.

G.5 Dependence on predictor availability

Targets M1 and M2 require the delivered predictors or the released pools from which they can be reconstructed. If the predictor artefacts are not recoverable from the benchmark’s public release, the programme restricts M1 and M2 to the reference and seed-only predictors, which are constructed from public data, and treats M3 as the primary evidence. This restriction is stated in advance and does not change the falsification criteria.

---

Appendix H. Pre-Registration and Falsification Protocol

H.1 Falsifiers

F1 (empirical adequacy of the heuristic). If the implicit tolerances  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}  lie within a factor of  1.5  of one another across the six campaigns, the benchmark’s NDCG calibration is empirically decision-coherent, and the programme pivots to a structural explanation of why.

F2 (class-level irrelevance of Proposition 1). If, on synthetic pools matched to each campaign and restricted to the reachable predictor class  \mathcal H , the gap  \Delta_{k_{\mathrm{dec}}}  is below the sampling noise on at least five of six campaigns, then the non-identifiability of Proposition 1 does not bite in the regime the benchmark operates, and the empirical contribution contracts to the tolerance table.

F3 (instability in  \rho ). If the decision-gate pass/fail ordering of the evaluated configurations reverses between  \rho=1/4  and  \rho=3/4  on more than two campaigns, then the decision tolerance dominates the verdict, and the programme reports the gate as tolerance-dependent rather than as a unique certification.

H.2 The pivot

If any two of F1–F3 trigger, the thesis becomes: a formal diagnosis of NDCG-as-learning-gate, together with evidence that the benchmark’s heuristic was adequate on its six campaigns for identifiable structural reasons. This is a legitimate and publishable outcome, written into the programme as Plan B.

H.3 Predictions separated from falsifiers

The programme predicts that  \varepsilon_c^{\mathrm{ASB}}  will exceed  \varepsilon_c(1/2)  on at least three of six campaigns—that the benchmark’s threshold is lenient in decision terms—with moderate confidence. It predicts that Proposition 4’s margin condition will hold on the separated-peak campaign and fail on the clustered-hit campaign. It does not predict per-configuration outcomes on  n=3  trial counts; such predictions are underpowered and are excluded from the pre-registration.

