Appendix I. The Reachable Predictor Class  \mathcal H 

I.1 Definition

The reachable class  \mathcal H  is the set of predictors that a campaign’s reference solution and training budget could plausibly produce. It is not the set of all measurable functions, nor the set of all predictors satisfying a global  \ell_\infty  bound. It is defined operationally as follows:

\mathcal H
=
\left\{
\hat f
:
\hat f \text{ is a posterior-mean or posterior-sample predictor}
\text{ from a surrogate fit to } \mathcal D_0 \cup \mathcal D_{\mathrm{measured}}
\right\},

where  \mathcal D_{\mathrm{measured}}  is a measurement set of size at most  Rb , the campaign’s budget.

I.2 Synthetic construction

For the gap-profile simulation (M3),  \mathcal H  is approximated by

\hat f = f_{\mathrm{bench}} + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2 I_m),

where  f_{\mathrm{bench}}  is the benchmark response and  \sigma  is chosen to match the campaign’s posterior uncertainty. This is a deliberately simplified model of the reachable class; it captures the essential feature that predictors are noisy versions of the true response, with noise scale calibrated to the measurement budget.

I.3 Why Proposition 1 is a metric statement

Proposition 1 uses predictors that need not lie in  \mathcal H . This is intentional: the proposition is a statement about the metric, not about the training procedure. The empirical question is whether the gap  \Delta_k  is non-negligible within  \mathcal H . This is precisely what M3 measures, and it is the reason F2 is a falsifier: if  \Delta_k  is below noise on at least five of six campaigns, the metric-level non-identifiability does not translate into class-level practical harm.

---

Appendix J. Computational Verification of Proposition 1 and  \Delta_k 

J.1 Proposition 1 verification

The following procedure reproduces the construction of Appendix A. No training, no physical experiments, and no code training are involved; the computation is arithmetic on the constructed vectors.

Algorithm J.1 (Proposition 1 verification).

```
Input: f = (10, 8, 3.17, 0, 0), k = 2, tau = 0
       f1_scores = (10, 0, 0, 8, 0)
       f2_scores = (0, 10, 9, 0, 0)

Procedure:
1. pi1 = argsort(-f1_scores); pi2 = argsort(-f2_scores)
2. DCG1 = sum_{i=1..k} rho(f[pi1[i]]) / log2(i+1)
   DCG2 = sum_{i=1..k} rho(f[pi2[i]]) / log2(i+1)
3. ideal_pi = argsort(-f)
   Z = sum_{i=1..k} rho(f[ideal_pi[i]]) / log2(i+1)
4. NDCG1 = DCG1 / Z; NDCG2 = DCG2 / Z
5. T1 = pi1[1..k]; T2 = pi2[1..k]
   R1 = (sum_{i in ideal_pi[1..k]} f[i] - sum_{i in T1} f[i]) / k
   R2 = (sum_{i in ideal_pi[1..k]} f[i] - sum_{i in T2} f[i]) / k
6. Assert NDCG1 ≈ NDCG2
   Assert R1 ≠ R2
```

The expected output is NDCG1 = NDCG2 ≈ 0.6646, R1 = 4, R2 = 3.415.

J.2 Gap functional simulation

The following procedure implements the lower-envelope construction of Appendix D.

Algorithm J.2 (Gap profile).

```
Input: f_bench (length m), k, N (number of predictors), sigma

Procedure:
1. For j = 1..N:
     f_hat_j = f_bench + Normal(0, sigma^2, size=m)
     ndcg_j = NDCG@k(f_hat_j, f_bench)
     regret_j = R_k(f_hat_j, f_bench)
2. Sort points by ndcg_j
3. Compute lower envelope E(t) = min{regret_j : ndcg_j >= t}
4. For each j:
     Delta_j = regret_j - E(ndcg_j)
5. Return (ndcg_j, regret_j, Delta_j)
```

The output is a scatter of NDCG versus regret, with  \Delta_k  as the excess regret above the lower envelope. This is the central empirical object of the gap-profile measurement (M3).

J.3 What the computation does not establish

The simulation in J.2 is not an experiment on ASB and does not use any trained model. It is a computational verification of the arithmetic of Proposition 1 and an illustration of the lower-envelope definition of  \Delta_k . The empirical claims of the programme require the measurement targets M1–M4 on the released benchmark artefacts, not the synthetic construction of Appendix J. The synthetic construction is included to make the definitions operational and to allow the gap profile to be visualised before the benchmark artefacts are ingested.

